<h1>heuristic</h1>
<p>
(algorithmic technique)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
An <a href="algorithm.html"><em>algorithm</em></a> that usually, but not always, works or that gives nearly the right answer.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="movefront.html"><em>move-to-front heuristic</em></a>, <a href="christofides.html"><em>Christofides algorithm</em></a>, <a href="monteCarlo.html"><em>Monte Carlo algorithm</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="metaheuristic.html"><em>metaheuristic</em></a>, <a href="probablAlgo.html"><em>probabilistic algorithm</em></a>, <a href="greedyalgo.html"><em>greedy algorithm</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Some problems, such as the <a href="travelingSalesman.html"><em>traveling salesman</em></a> problem, may take far too long to compute an <a href="optimalsoltn.html"><em>optimal solution</em></a>.  But some have good heuristics that are fast and find a solution no more than a few percent worse than the optimal.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

<hr>

